 "title": "Second half of 2026 property tax payments due Oct. 15 | Ramsey County, Minnesota",
 "text": "Second half of 2026 property tax payments due Oct. 15 | Ramsey County, Minnesota Skip to main content Language\nHome Jobs News & Updates Calendar Contact Us\nResidents Residents Overview\nAssistance & Support\nElections & Voting\nEmergency Response & Preparedness\nEnvironment\nHealth & Medical\nLibraries\nLicenses, Permits & Records\nParks & Recreation\nProperty & Home\nPublic Safety & Law\nRecycling & Waste\nRoads & Transportation\nEvent Spaces\nBusinesses Businesses Overview\nDoing Business with Ramsey County\nWorkforce & Business Development\nLicenses, Permits & Inspections\nProperty & Development\nRecycling & Waste\nTransit Sales & Use Tax\nYour Government Your Government Overview\nLeadership\nAbout Ramsey County\nContacts & Locations\nDepartments\nProjects & Initiatives\nOrdinances & Regulations\nVolunteer\nOpen Government\nHome /\nNews /\nSecond half of 2026 property tax payments due Oct. 15\nOctober 8, 2026\nSecond half of 2026 property tax payments due Oct. 15\nProperty taxes and values\nProperty Tax, Records & Election Services\nThe second half of 2026 property tax payments are due Thursday, Oct. 15. If your tax is $100 or less for real property or $50 or less for personal property or manufactured homes, it must be paid in full by Oct. 15.\nPayment options\n​ Payments will be accepted via U.S. mail, at secure form drop-off locations and electronically. An online payment portal for property tax is available now.\nPay online\nPay by mail, in person, or drop box\nPay by bill pay\nPayments must be postmarked on or before Oct. 15 to avoid penalty. Checks and money orders should be made payable to Ramsey County.\nA one-time late payment penalty waiver program is available to taxpayers. It may used once every ten years. It applies even if a property owner has used the one-time waiver in the past, and it will not impact the ability to use the waiver in the future.\nMore information on property tax payments\nUnderstanding your property taxes\nWatch a video to learn more about how taxes are determined, how to read your statement and how to resolve questions. The video is available in five languages:\nEnglish: Understanding Your Property Taxes\nHmoob: Totaub Txog Cov Se Tsev thiab Lub Tsev Muaj Nqis Li Cas\nKaren: Understanding Your Property Taxes and Values\nSoomaali: Fahamka Cashuuraha iyo Qiimaha Hantidaada Maguurtada\nEspañol: Comprendiendo los Impuestos y Valores de Su Propiedad\nRelated news\nNew economic development plan invests more than $320 million into the Ramsey County’s future\nThe Building Stronger Together initiative seeks to increase investment in downtown Saint Paul and reduce the property tax burden throughout the county.\nApril 14, 2026\nPress Release, Economic development, Property taxes and values\nRead more\n2026 Assessor's Report available online\nThe report details changes and trends in assessed property values throughout Ramsey County, based on type and location. \nApril 1, 2026\nProperty taxes and values\nRead more\nBack to top\nConnect with Ramsey County\nA county of excellence working with you to enhance our quality of life.\nContact us Sign up for email updates\nResidents Assistance & Support Elections & Voting Emergency Response & Preparedness Environment Health & Medical Libraries Licenses, Permits & Records Parks & Recreation Property & Home Public Safety & Law Recycling & Waste Roads & Transportation Event Spaces\nBusinesses Doing Business with Ramsey County Workforce & Business Development Licenses, Permits & Inspections Property & Development Recycling & Waste Transit Sales & Use Tax\nYour Government Leadership About Ramsey County Contacts & Locations Departments Projects & Initiatives Ordinances & Regulations Volunteer Open Government\n© 2026 Ramsey County, MN. All rights reserved.\nTerms of Use\nContact Us\nFor Employees\nWeb Accessibility\nPowered by Interpersonal Frequency",
